  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mayaheros-urophthalmus",
      "html": "<p>The Mayan cichlid, Mayaheros urophthalmus, is a large cichlid native to eastern parts of Mexico, Belize, Guatemala, Honduras and Nicaragua. Also known as the Mexican mojarra, it lives in swamps, marshes and coastal lagoons, can tolerate and even breed in full marine conditions, and is often seen spawning around mangroves. Territorial and fairly aggressive, it is regarded as a potential pest fish in the USA, where introduced populations exist. It is still often sold as Cichlasoma urophthalmus.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mayaheros-urophthalmus",
      "html": "<p>Its natural range encompasses eastern parts of several Central American countries, including Mexico, Belize, Guatemala, Honduras and Nicaragua. Like several other members of the genus, introduced populations are known to exist in both the USA and Singapore.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mayaheros-urophthalmus",
      "html": "<p>Chiefly inhabits coastal plains and surrounding lowlands, where it lives in sluggish waters such as swamps, marshes or large ponds. It can also tolerate brackish conditions and is often seen living and even spawning around mangrove swamps and coastal lagoons.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mayaheros-urophthalmus",
      "html": "<p>Standard length: 400 mm SL. Aquarium base: 210 × 60 cm. Volume: ~756 litres (210 × 60 × 60 cm).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mayaheros-urophthalmus",
      "html": "<p>Can be kept in fresh water but actually appreciates brackish conditions. Make sure there is a lot of swimming area for this species, but also provide cover in the form of stable rocks and bits of wood. It is a known digger so plants won't last long, and because of its size potted plants likely won't either. Floating plants are an option, as are plants rooted to bits of wood or rock.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mayaheros-urophthalmus",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 20–30 °C</li><li>pH: 7.0–8.0</li><li>Hardness: up to 20 dGH</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mayaheros-urophthalmus",
      "html": "<p>In nature it feeds primarily on aquatic invertebrates although stomach analyses of wild specimens have shown that it also ingests a fair amount of algae and other plant matter. It's unknown if the fish in question had consumed these directly or via the gut contents of other prey items. Not a fussy eater in captivity. Feed a good cichlid pellet as a staple diet supplemented with meaty foods such as prawn and mussel.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mayaheros-urophthalmus",
      "html": "<p>Territorial and fairly aggressive. This species is only suitable for a community of medium to large fish, such as other cichlids, robust characins and catfish. There is no guarantee of success in trying this approach however; the more fish in the tank, the more the aggression is likely to be dispersed. Ensure sufficient decor is provided to allow all the fish to claim a territory. Can also be kept as a species-only tank with a bonded pair.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mayaheros-urophthalmus",
      "html": "<p>Male is the larger fish, is more colourful and has extended dorsal and anal fins.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mayaheros-urophthalmus",
      "html": "<p>Few details of breeding in the hobby but likely to be similar to Cichlasoma species and their relatives.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/mayaheros-urophthalmus",
      "html": "<p>A hardy and robust fish that can tolerate and breed in full marine conditions. The Mayan cichlid is also referred to as the Mexican mojarra and is occasionally offered for sale in the UK, or can be obtained through specialist cichlid dealers. This species is regarded as a potential pest fish in the USA due to its tolerance of a wide range of conditions and the threat it could pose to native fauna.</p>"
    }
  ],
